response:
 The sum is 11. If the sum is $n$, placing 1 in the top left triangle yields a configuration as shown: [asy]
size(101);
draw(unitsquare);
draw(shift(up)*shift(right)*unitsquare);
filldraw(shift(up)*unitsquare,gray(.6));
filldraw(shift(right)*unitsquare,gray(.6));
draw((0,0)--(2,2)^^(0,2)--(2,0));
label("1",(1,1));
[/asy]The sum of the numbers in the top row and the sum of the numbers in the left column are both $n+1$. Hence, the sum of all these four numbers is $2n+1$. Since the sum of all the numbers from 2 to 9 is also $2n+1$, it is possible to replace 1 with 9 in the figure and have it remain correctly positioned. However, if 1 is replaced with 2, it is not possible to keep the figure correct. Therefore, the sum is 11.</think> <answer> 11 </answer>

